WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.960
He was a comic genius who had only one speed
- full throttle.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.280
From the moment he burst into our lives as
an alien on Happy Days, he amazed us with

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:12.249
a range of talent that left us breathless.

00:00:12.249 --> 00:00:17.600
Yet, behind the manic shtick of Robin Williams
was a man who was deeply plagued by depression.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:20.950
He had other demons, such as alcohol and cocaine.

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:25.320
Still he kept us entertained to the very end,
which made it all the more unfathomable to

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:29.420
his legion of fans and admirers when he took
his life, at the age of 63.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:41.540
In today’s Biographics, we get to the essence
of the real Robin Williams.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:42.540
* * *

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:52.340
Robin McLaurin Williams came into the world
on July 21st, 1951.

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:57.870
Born at St. Luke's Hospital in Chicago, Illinois
he was the only child of Laurie and Robert

00:00:57.870 --> 00:00:58.870
Williams.

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:03.090
The family was wealthy, with Robert being
a senior executive at the Ford Motor Company.

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:05.700
Laurie was a former model and part-time actress.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:10.770
Much of Robin’s formative years were spent
alone in the huge mansion that was his home.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:15.080
In order to cope with his loneliness he created
characters and conversations in his head.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:18.100
He was an overweight child, which led to bullying
at school.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:22.310
The sixth grade, he later recalled, was the
most difficult year of his life.

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:26.170
Many days he went home crying, after constant
name calling and physical harassment.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:30.430
Each day was a torment for him, with the only
friends the ones he created in his imagination.

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:33.940
The hardship of these early years brought
on a deep seated state of depression that

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:36.910
Robin was to grapple with for the rest of
his life.

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:39.560
Robin began to use comedy as a defense mechanism.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.290
It started with his mother.

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:46.070
Laurie had a very sharp wit and would often
use humor, laced with sarcasm, to communicate

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:47.070
with her son.

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:50.070
In order to connect with her, Robin had to
make his mother laugh.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:53.720
It wasn’t good enough to be Robin; he had
to be funny Robin.

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:57.930
By the time he was 16, the family had relocated
to Tiburon, California where Robin attended

00:01:57.930 --> 00:01:59.000
Redwood High School.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:02.610
It was the late sixties, and the straight
laced Robin found himself in the middle of

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:06.090
a counterculture that revolved around drugs
and sex.

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:10.399
He was able to loosen up and, after dabbling
with marijuana, found himself fitting in for

00:02:10.399 --> 00:02:11.400
the first time.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.930
It was at Redwood High that Robin stumbled
upon the drama department.

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:20.239
He had already perfected his comedic veneer,
quickly establishing a reputation as the class

00:02:20.239 --> 00:02:21.239
clown.

00:02:21.239 --> 00:02:26.250
He fell in love with the idea of improv performance,
his comedic personality slowly emerging through

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:27.310
drama classes.

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:32.400
At the same time, Robin got involved in wrestling
and soccer, proving to be an accomplished

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:33.400
athlete.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:38.560
Yet, by the time he graduated college in 1969,
he was voted by his classmates as the ‘least

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.060
likely to succeed.’

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:43.390
Not surprisingly he was also voted ‘funniest
student.’

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:47.930
After graduation, Robin attended Claremont
Men’s College, his sights set on a career

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:49.709
in political science.

00:02:49.709 --> 00:02:54.320
Before long, however, he was spending more
time in the college theater than in lecture

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:55.320
rooms.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:59.239
He won the coveted role of Fagin in the College’s
production of Oliver, turning in a stellar

00:02:59.239 --> 00:03:02.860
performance that made it clear that he was
destined for the limelight.

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:07.880
Leaving behind his diplomatic ambitions, Robin
enrolled in a community college theater program.

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:12.700
During classes he would often improvise, leaving
his classmates and teachers in fits of laughter.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:17.160
After three years of honing his craft, he
was offered the opportunity of a lifetime

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:21.560
- a full scholarship to the Juilliard School
of Performing Arts in New York City.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:27.421
Williams was one of only two students accepted
into the 1973 advance program at Juilliard.

00:03:27.421 --> 00:03:30.180
The other was future superman actor, Christopher
Reeve.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:35.010
Reeve, who would become a close friend, recalled
his first impressions upon meeting Williams:

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:38.599
He wore tie-dyed shirts with tracksuit bottoms
and talked a mile a minute.

00:03:38.599 --> 00:03:41.650
I’d never seen so much energy contained
in one person.

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:45.959
He was like an untied balloon that had been
inflated and immediately released.

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:50.140
I watched in awe as he virtually caromed off
the walls of the class and hallways.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:54.110
To say that he was ‘on’ would be a major
understatement.

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:58.319
At Juilliard, Robin astounded his teachers
and fellow students alike.

00:03:58.319 --> 00:04:03.140
He was able to take on the persona of characters
with ease, trading out one accent or dialect

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:05.920
for another like he was born to do it.

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:08.400
However, Robin was frustrated at Juilliard.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:09.909
He just didn’t fit in.

00:04:09.909 --> 00:04:13.370
After two years, his teachers told him that
there was nothing more that they could teach

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:14.370
him.

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:17.559
One of his instructors considered Robin to
be a genius who was not being properly served

00:04:17.559 --> 00:04:20.859
by the school’s conservative and classical
training style.

00:04:20.859 --> 00:04:25.090
He left Juilliard in 1976, ready to launch
himself on the world.

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:31.009
His first stop was the stand up circuit on
the West Coast.

00:04:31.009 --> 00:04:34.339
Comic

00:04:34.339 --> 00:04:42.319
Robin’s first break into the realms of comedy
came at a club in the San Francisco Bay Area.

00:04:42.319 --> 00:04:47.110
His first gig was at the Holy City Zoo, where
he also spent time tending bar.

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:50.349
These early sets were frenetic, bordering
on psychotic.

00:04:50.349 --> 00:04:53.749
No-one had ever seen anything quite like Robin
Williams.

00:04:53.749 --> 00:04:59.110
He played a collection of edgy characters
including Reverend Oral Satisfaction.

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:03.580
His early stand-up was influenced by the likes
of Lenny Bruce, Peter Sellers and Jonathan

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:04.580
Winters.

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:09.969
His manic edge, quick fire ad-lib routines
and off-the -wall physical comedy proved a

00:05:09.969 --> 00:05:13.039
hit and he soon established a loyal following.

00:05:13.039 --> 00:05:17.249
Robin loved the immediate feedback that came
from stand-up, and it was something that he

00:05:17.249 --> 00:05:19.470
would continue to do right through his career.

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:24.270
Despite the apparent ease of his performances,
he actually found stand-up very stressful.

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:28.240
Underneath the cloak of his act, he was an
intensely shy man.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:33.189
He once said that he started using drugs and
alcohol as a crutch to combat his stage nerves.

00:05:33.189 --> 00:05:37.909
In 1977, Williams moved to Los Angeles where
he began performing at the Comedy Club.

00:05:37.909 --> 00:05:42.770
It was here that he was discovered by TV producer
George Schlatter who offered him a spot on

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:46.520
a revival of the old 60’s show Laugh In.

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:51.930
The show didn’t survive, but for Williams
it established a foothold into a career in

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:52.930
television.

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:57.199
He became a semi-regular cast member on The
Richard Pryor Show, while also continuing

00:05:57.199 --> 00:05:58.740
to perform stand-up.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:02.229
It was an appearance on The Richard Pryor
Show that brought Williams to the attention

00:06:02.229 --> 00:06:05.909
of Gary Marshall, the producer of the hit
sit-com Happy Days.

00:06:05.909 --> 00:06:09.540
After five seasons, the scriptwriters were
seriously running out of ideas (though they

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:11.520
hadn’t yet ‘jumped the shark’).

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:16.460
They decided to do an episode taking off the
1960’s sci-fi comedy My Favorite Martian.

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:20.129
The episode was called My Favorite Orkan and
called for the casting of an actor to play

00:06:20.129 --> 00:06:22.029
an alien from the land of Ork.

00:06:22.029 --> 00:06:25.039
Marshall though that Williams might be the
man for the job.

00:06:25.039 --> 00:06:29.929
In what has gone down as possibly the most
brilliant audition in TV history, Robin entered

00:06:29.929 --> 00:06:32.589
the room and immediately sat in the chair
on his head.

00:06:32.589 --> 00:06:35.999
He proceeded to do his lines, and then broke
into an inspired ad-lib.

00:06:35.999 --> 00:06:40.999
Marshal cast him on the spot, later commenting
that Williams was the only alien who turned

00:06:40.999 --> 00:06:41.999
up for the part.

00:06:41.999 --> 00:06:45.970
The episode of Happy Days was originally shot
as a dream sequence on the part of Richie

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:46.970
Cunningham.

00:06:46.970 --> 00:06:50.500
However, the character played by Williams,
Mork from Ork, was such a hit with the TV

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:55.379
watching audience after an initial broadcast,
that the ending was re-filmed so that Mork

00:06:55.379 --> 00:06:56.689
was a real character.

00:06:56.689 --> 00:07:01.309
Much of Robin’s work on the Happy Days episode
was improvised and augmented with physical

00:07:01.309 --> 00:07:02.309
comedy.

00:07:02.309 --> 00:07:03.309
He spoke in a high, nasally voice, set off
buy his signature greeting ‘nanoo-nanoo.’

00:07:03.309 --> 00:07:06.249
Audiences couldn’t get enough of the zany
alien and demanded more.

00:07:06.249 --> 00:07:12.279
This led to the creation of the spin-off series
Mork &amp; Mindy, which ran from 1978 to 1982.

00:07:12.279 --> 00:07:16.629
The show catapulted Williams to super stardom
literally overnight.

00:07:16.629 --> 00:07:21.249
After just two weeks on the air, it had zoomed
to the top 10 of the Nielsen ratings.

00:07:21.249 --> 00:07:24.689
The producers of the show kept the script
loose enough to give Robin plenty of room

00:07:24.689 --> 00:07:29.800
to do as he pleased - and the results were
unpredictable, over-the-top and hilarious.

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:34.939
While the critics weren’t overly enthused
with the show, they were falling over themselves

00:07:34.939 --> 00:07:40.229
to lavish praise upon its star.

00:07:40.229 --> 00:07:46.870
Super Star

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:50.330
Mork &amp; Mindy quickly became a pop culture
phenomenon.

00:07:50.330 --> 00:07:54.369
A generation of kids took to wearing Mork’s
trademark rainbow suspenders and greeting

00:07:54.369 --> 00:07:56.759
their friends with the words ‘nanoo-nanoo.’

00:07:56.759 --> 00:08:01.539
Robin went from relative obscurity to instant
fame in the space of a couple of months.

00:08:01.539 --> 00:08:02.679
It was something that he wasn’t ready for.

00:08:02.679 --> 00:08:09.759
He began indulging in the excesses that come
with fame - drugs, alcohol and women.

00:08:09.759 --> 00:08:14.680
Just as he acted in the fast lane, so to did
he live his live in the very fastest of lanes.

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:18.841
He quickly slipped into the habit of partying
into the early hours, crawling home at 5 in

00:08:18.841 --> 00:08:21.809
the morning and then having to be on the set
by 10.

00:08:21.809 --> 00:08:25.979
There is no doubt that Robin was blessed with
more physical energy than most.

00:08:25.979 --> 00:08:29.059
Inevitably, however, that energy began to
burn out.

00:08:29.059 --> 00:08:33.550
He would sometimes fall asleep on the set,
or just sit dormant in ‘off’ mode.

00:08:33.550 --> 00:08:37.569
One night, after performing a late night set
at a comedy club, Robin met up with fellow

00:08:37.569 --> 00:08:38.740
comic John Belushi.

00:08:38.740 --> 00:08:43.110
They ended up at the Chateau Marmont, where
Williams shared some of Belushi’s cocaine

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:44.199
and then left.

00:08:44.199 --> 00:08:48.509
The next day, on the set of Mork &amp; Mindy,
co-star Pam Dawber broke the news to Williams

00:08:48.509 --> 00:08:50.149
that Belushi had died of a drug overdose.

00:08:50.149 --> 00:08:51.149
Robin was devastated.

00:08:51.149 --> 00:08:54.830
The news shocked him into making changes in
his lifestyle.

00:08:54.830 --> 00:08:59.410
The death of Belushi tarnished the image of
Hollywood’s fast living brat pack.

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:03.269
Robin was called to testify before a grand
jury, an experience which, along with the

00:09:03.269 --> 00:09:07.120
shock of Belushi’s death and the birth of
his first child, Zak, allowed him to break

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:09.820
free from the stranglehold of drugs and alcohol.

00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:14.540
It was at this time that he discovered cycling,
a form of exercise which he credits for saving

00:09:14.540 --> 00:09:15.540
his life.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:19.129
After four years, Mork &amp; Mindy had lost its
midas touch.

00:09:19.129 --> 00:09:23.740
Producers tried whatever they could to resurrect
the initial appeal but nothing seemed to work.

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:27.750
They decided to make Mork less naive, allowing
Williams to work in more of his nightclub

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:28.750
act material.

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:33.209
Interestingly, the Mork character also found
its way into his stand up routines, though

00:09:33.209 --> 00:09:35.079
not by Robin’s design.

00:09:35.079 --> 00:09:39.430
Audience members at his late night shows would
yell out, ‘Do Mork,’ to which Robin would

00:09:39.430 --> 00:09:41.550
frustratingly respond, ‘Mork’s not here.

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:43.019
I’m a different guy!’

00:09:43.019 --> 00:09:47.759
By the middle of 1982, the character that
had propelled Williams to superstar status

00:09:47.759 --> 00:09:50.900
was beginning to feel like a millstone around
his neck.

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:55.110
So, it came as somewhat of a relief to him
when Mork &amp; Mindy was cancelled after four

00:09:55.110 --> 00:09:58.510
seasons.

00:09:58.510 --> 00:10:05.319
Family Man

00:10:05.319 --> 00:10:10.580
In 1976, Robin met Valerie Velardi while working
as a bartender in San Francisco, shortly after

00:10:10.580 --> 00:10:11.709
leaving Juilliard.

00:10:11.709 --> 00:10:14.180
She was a dancer, having studied at Juilliard
herself.

00:10:14.180 --> 00:10:19.459
They began a relationship and, after two years,
were married on June 4th, 1978.

00:10:19.459 --> 00:10:24.790
Valerie endured her husband’s cocaine addiction
and wild partying lifestyle for a number of

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:25.810
years.

00:10:25.810 --> 00:10:30.830
It was with the birth of their son, Zachary,
on April 11th, 1983 that he finally managed

00:10:30.830 --> 00:10:33.470
to shake off his addiction.

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:37.329
However, he was unable to control his wandering
eye.

00:10:37.329 --> 00:10:41.899
He ended up having an affair with a cocktail
waitress by the name of Michelle Tish Carter.

00:10:41.899 --> 00:10:47.899
In 1986, Miss Carter sued Williams for $6.4
million, claiming that he had knowingly infected

00:10:47.899 --> 00:10:50.459
her with herpes simplex virus.

00:10:50.459 --> 00:10:54.480
He settled out of court, only for his wife
to sue for divorce a few months later.

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:59.500
On April 30th, 1989, less than a year after
the divorce from Valerie being finalized,

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:03.300
Williams married Marcia Grimes, the former
nanny to his son, Zach.

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:07.230
They had actually been carrying on an affair
during the last two years of his previous

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:08.230
marriage.

00:11:08.230 --> 00:11:11.970
At the time of the wedding, Marcia was pregnant
with Robin’s child, a daughter who they

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:13.460
named Zelda Rae.

00:11:13.460 --> 00:11:16.450
Two years later, they had a second child,
Cody Alan.

00:11:16.450 --> 00:11:22.029
The marriage endured for 19 years, with Marcia
finally leaving, unable to handle Robin’s

00:11:22.029 --> 00:11:23.420
relapse into addiction.

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:27.699
Williams married for the third and final time
in 2011.

00:11:27.699 --> 00:11:30.040
Susan Schneider was a graphic designer at
the time.

00:11:30.040 --> 00:11:33.839
He met her while recovering from a heart operation
in 2009.

00:11:33.839 --> 00:11:40.509
They were still together at the time of Robin’s
death in 2014.

00:11:40.509 --> 00:11:45.220
Movie Star

00:11:45.220 --> 00:11:51.749
Robin’s fame on the small screen inevitably
aroused the interest of movie makers.

00:11:51.749 --> 00:11:55.480
His first starring role came in 1980’s Popeye.

00:11:55.480 --> 00:11:59.670
The movie was a financial and critical flop
and it looked like Williams’ film career

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:02.689
was over before he’d even gotten out of
the gate.

00:12:02.689 --> 00:12:07.120
But then, in 1982’s The World According
to Garp, he was able to show more of his dramatic

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:08.199
acting talents.

00:12:08.199 --> 00:12:13.210
The film was well received , with critic Roger
Ebert calling Robin’s performance ‘unconventional

00:12:13.210 --> 00:12:14.210
and absorbing.’

00:12:14.210 --> 00:12:18.860
Following a couple of smaller roles he got
his big screen break in 1987’s Good Morning

00:12:18.860 --> 00:12:19.860
Vietnam.

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:25.709
The role of Adrian Cronauer, a real life Armed
Forces DJ stationed in Saigon during the war,

00:12:25.709 --> 00:12:28.199
was tailor made for Williams’ manic style.

00:12:28.199 --> 00:12:30.890
In fact, nearly everything that he did on
screen was improvised.

00:12:30.890 --> 00:12:36.079
The performance was good enough to earn him
an Academy Award nomination for Best Actor.

00:12:36.079 --> 00:12:40.170
Over the next decade, Williams appeared in
an impressive number of films, displaying

00:12:40.170 --> 00:12:43.370
a huge range and appeal across the generations.

00:12:43.370 --> 00:12:48.399
He shone as both a dramatic and a comic actor,
with his first and only Oscar coming for Best

00:12:48.399 --> 00:12:52.779
Supporting Actor playing a psychologist in
1997’s Good Will Hunting.

00:12:52.779 --> 00:12:58.470
He had previously received two nominations,
for 1988’s Dead Poet’s Society and 1991’s

00:12:58.470 --> 00:12:59.470
The Fisher King.

00:12:59.470 --> 00:13:04.589
Perhaps Williams’ most versatile role was
one in which he was never actually on screen.

00:13:04.589 --> 00:13:08.459
His role as the genie in the 1992 Disney animated
musical Aladdin showcased his amazing vocal

00:13:08.459 --> 00:13:09.459
range.

00:13:09.459 --> 00:13:12.910
The role had been specifically written for
him, although he improvised much of the dialogue,

00:13:12.910 --> 00:13:14.930
just as he had done in Good Morning Vietnam.

00:13:14.930 --> 00:13:19.459
Williams played what would prove to be his
most beloved character in 1993’s Mrs Doubtfire,

00:13:19.459 --> 00:13:24.509
in which he portrayed a recently divorced
actor who takes on the persona of an elderly

00:13:24.509 --> 00:13:27.250
Scottish housemaid to get closer to his children.

00:13:27.250 --> 00:13:31.390
He spent four hours every day putting on his
makeup to play the part.

00:13:31.390 --> 00:13:35.970
The movie was a great success and a sequel
was in the works at the time of Williams’

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:37.939
death in 2014.

00:13:37.939 --> 00:13:41.439
His darkest role came in 2002’s One Hour
Photo.

00:13:41.439 --> 00:13:45.610
Williams played a lonely photo technician
who becomes obsessed with a family whose photos

00:13:45.610 --> 00:13:47.360
he has developed for years.

00:13:47.360 --> 00:13:52.279
He received rave reviews from such esteemed
critics as Leonard Maltin and Roger Ebert.

00:13:52.279 --> 00:13:56.850
Robin’s last appearance on the big screen
was in 2014’s Night at the Museum: Secret

00:13:56.850 --> 00:14:00.420
of the Tomb, in which he reprised his role
as President Theodore Roosevelt.

00:14:00.420 --> 00:14:05.190
In the 2015 animated film Absolutely Anything
he provided the voice of Dennis the Dog.

00:14:05.190 --> 00:14:12.399
It closed out a 38 year career that encompassed
74 movies.

00:14:12.399 --> 00:14:14.480
Philanthropist

00:14:14.480 --> 00:14:24.139
In 1996, together with fellow celebrities
Whoopi Goldberg and Billy Crystal, Robin founded

00:14:24.139 --> 00:14:25.139
Comic Relief, USA.

00:14:25.139 --> 00:14:30.630
The charity helped the homeless, with $80
million being raised as of last year.

00:14:30.630 --> 00:14:34.230
Together with his second wife, he also created
the Windfall Foundation to help donate money

00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:38.499
to a variety of causes, including St. Jude
Children’s Research Hospital.

00:14:38.499 --> 00:14:44.930
On May 27th, 1995 Robin’s close friend Christopher
Reeve was thrown from a horse and paralysed.

00:14:44.930 --> 00:14:47.870
Robin dropped everything and rushed to his
bedside.

00:14:47.870 --> 00:14:52.509
He turned up in full costume as a crazed Russian
doctor, giving Reeve the first laugh since

00:14:52.509 --> 00:14:54.089
his tragic accident.

00:14:54.089 --> 00:14:58.220
Robin would go on to to donate huge sums to
research into spinal cord injury.

00:14:58.220 --> 00:15:02.120
When Reeves died in 2004, Robin was devastated.

00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:04.890
Williams was also passionate about supporting
the military.

00:15:04.890 --> 00:15:08.730
He traveled and performed at so many bases
around the globe that he was known as the

00:15:08.730 --> 00:15:10.269
modern day Bob Hope.

00:15:10.269 --> 00:15:14.410
During his career, he entertained over a hundred
thousand troops in thirteen countries

00:15:14.410 --> 00:15:23.579
Addict

00:15:23.579 --> 00:15:26.949
Williams managed to quit alcohol and cocaine
in the early 1980’s.

00:15:26.949 --> 00:15:32.360
However, in 2003 he lapsed back into alcohol
abuse while working on the movie The Big White

00:15:32.360 --> 00:15:33.639
on location in Alaska.

00:15:33.639 --> 00:15:36.749
For the next three years he denied that he
had a problem.

00:15:36.749 --> 00:15:40.550
Finally, he was confronted by family and friends,
who convinced him to check himself into a

00:15:40.550 --> 00:15:44.199
substance-abuse rehabilitation center in Newberg,
Oregon.

00:15:44.199 --> 00:15:47.059
There he admitted that he was an alcoholic.

00:15:47.059 --> 00:15:51.759
After sixty days in rehab, rather than returning
to his family, Robin began living with a sober

00:15:51.759 --> 00:15:55.509
companion, who was charged with ensuring that
he didn’t relapse.

00:15:55.509 --> 00:15:57.481
Shortly after that his second wife, Marcia
left him.

00:15:57.481 --> 00:16:02.390
A depressed Robin did what he always did when
life got too tough - he threw himself into

00:16:02.390 --> 00:16:05.009
his work, going on tour to do stand up.

00:16:05.009 --> 00:16:08.959
But in the middle of the tour it was discovered
that he needed heart surgery.

00:16:08.959 --> 00:16:13.019
The operation went well, but it required quiet
rest time to recuperate.

00:16:13.019 --> 00:16:17.959
This forced time of inactivity allowed the
demons in his head to have free reign.

00:16:17.959 --> 00:16:22.779
William’s addictive personality led to marathon
cycling and video gaming sessions.

00:16:22.779 --> 00:16:26.279
Cycling was a healthy obsession for him, but,
for a man who had struggled with depression

00:16:26.279 --> 00:16:30.519
his whole life, being alone in a room with
a computer was very dangerous.

00:16:30.519 --> 00:16:32.550
He soon began drinking again.

00:16:32.550 --> 00:16:35.199
This time he didn’t care who knew.

00:16:35.199 --> 00:16:40.319
In May of 2014, William’s new TV show, The
Crazy Ones, was cancelled after just one season.

00:16:40.319 --> 00:16:42.639
In July, he was back in rehab.

00:16:42.639 --> 00:16:45.759
Around that time he also learned that he had
Parkinson’s Disease.

00:16:45.759 --> 00:16:49.589
After his death it was discovered what had
been diagnosed as Parkinson’s was actually

00:16:49.589 --> 00:16:51.329
Lewy body dementia.

00:16:51.329 --> 00:16:55.089
After his death, Robin’s wife Susan revealed
that Robin had been been having severe and

00:16:55.089 --> 00:17:01.459
prolonged episodes of fear and anxiety, insomnia,
constipation, stress and hand tremors for

00:17:01.459 --> 00:17:02.459
over a year.

00:17:02.459 --> 00:17:06.110
She said that he would constantly tell her,
‘I just want to reboot my brain.’

00:17:06.110 --> 00:17:11.520
On August 11th, 2014, Robin Williams committed
suicide by hanging himself with a belt.

00:17:11.520 --> 00:17:13.030
He died from asphyxiation.

00:17:13.030 --> 00:17:17.380
His wife, Susan, stated that he was killed
by the ‘terrorist inside my husband’s

00:17:17.380 --> 00:17:18.380
brain.’

00:17:18.380 --> 00:17:22.579
Within hours of the tragic news of his passing,
thousands of fans were posting images of themselves

00:17:22.579 --> 00:17:27.110
standing on their desks with the caption,
‘ O’ Captain, my captain,’ in reference

00:17:27.110 --> 00:17:29.830
to Robin’s character in ‘Dead Poet’s
Society.’

00:17:29.830 --> 00:17:57.900
It was a fitting tribute.

